/-!
# Lir: a shared low-level IR with control flow, memory, calls and spilling

Lir is the intended common target of source compilers (such as the WebAssembly
lowering) and common source of ISA backends. It contains no architecture
policy.

* `Syntax`: 64-bit words; register operations (arithmetic, 64- and 32-bit
  shifts/rotates/division/comparisons, `clz`/`ctz`/`popcnt`, explicit sign and
  zero extension, full 64-bit constants); 1/2/4/8-byte little-endian loads and
  stores with zero/sign extension from one linear byte memory; stack-frame slots;
  calls with register-or-slot argument/result locations; and block terminators
  `jump`, `branch`, `ret`, `trap`. Programs are function tables of block lists.
* `Semantics`: `step : Prog R → State R → Result R` with an explicit call stack.
  `Result` distinguishes running, returned (values and memory), trapped (memory)
  and stuck. Division by zero, signed division overflow, out-of-bounds memory
  access and reads of unwritten registers or slots are stuck (undefined), not
  traps. `Behaves.unique` and `Behaves.exists` show that each state has exactly
  one behaviour among returned, trapped, stuck and diverging (an
  `InfiniteTrace` of steps). A fuel-bounded runner and a bridge to
  `Machine.Execution` are included.
* `Simulation`: a forward simulation from Lir to any target transition relation,
  with a measure excluding infinite stuttering, concluding that returned, trapped
  and diverging Lir executions are matched by target executions
  (`ForwardSimulation.returns`, `traps`, `diverges`). `CaseSimulation` states the
  obligations per transition kind.
* `Alloc`: spilling from virtual to physical registers with two scratch
  registers. `spill_behaves` proves preservation of every non-stuck behaviour,
  for every program and every allocation satisfying `Allocation.Valid`.
  `firstK` is a simple valid allocation.
* `Examples`: a factorial loop with an arbitrary-input theorem (also after
  spilling), a memory sum, recursive factorial, slot-passed arguments, guarded
  division and operator spot checks.
* `Absint.Check`: certified abstract interpretation. A decidable checker for an
  untrusted post-fixpoint (intervals, pointer offsets into parameter buffers,
  comparison facts for branch refinement) on a leaf function, and
  `check_sound`: every load and store of every run lies inside a declared
  buffer. `Absint.ParseHex4O1`/`O2` apply it to cJSON's `parse_hex4` lifted
  from gcc `-O1`/`-O2` machine code (the lift itself is tested, not checked).

Not provided: a liveness-based allocator; memory growth; globals, tables and
indirect calls; floating point; any backend lowering from Lir. Memory has a
single fixed size and addresses are unbounded naturals, so a backend has to
relate them to its own address space.
-/
